call_details.response:
The current board is completely symmetric about column 3, and columns 2–4 are already full. Consequently the only legal moves are the four edge columns 0, 1, 5 and 6 (symmetric pairs {0,6} and {1,5}).

No placement of an x produces an immediate four-in-a-row, nor does any placement create a direct immediate threat (a square that would be a winning move on the subsequent turn). The same statement holds for o, so the position contains no one-move forced win or forced block.

The only remaining winning squares that can be reached before the board fills are the two “mutual” cells (2,1) and (2,5). Each of those cells completes a four-in-a-row for whichever player occupies it:
- for x it finishes the horizontal line already occupied by the three x’s on row 2;
- for o it finishes one of the two long diagonals of o’s that already exist.

Reaching either cell requires exactly two further placements in its column (the cells on rows 4 and 3). All other conceivable fours (verticals on the outer columns, the few still-open diagonals that touch columns 0/1 or 5/6, \ldots) need at least as many moves and are easily obstructed.

Consequently the game reduces to a pure parity contest on the 14 “safe” squares that do not yet arm a mutual cell (the 12 squares of columns 0 and 6 together with the two dead cells on row 4 of columns 1 and 5). Second player can always answer a move inside a side column by replying in the same column and can answer a dead-cell move by taking the remaining dead cell. Under that pairing strategy:
- every side column receives strictly alternating colours, so no vertical four can ever appear;
- all 14 safe squares are exhausted after an even number of moves;
- first player is then forced to arm one of the mutual cells;
- second player takes that cell and wins.

Because the position is a forced loss with perfect play, every legal move is equivalent. The move that stays closest to the centre (and therefore leaves the greatest number of practical chances should the opponent later deviate from the pairing strategy) is column 1.

Final Answer: 1

